<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
          xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- The release source set states the offline guarantee a second time, on purpose.
         main/ already carries the same element, and main/ is what strips a grant merged
         in from a plugin AAR. This file is the one an auditor diffs: it says, in the
         build type that actually ships, that CatchLaw asks for no network permission.

         SPEC.md §11 prints exactly this file. Nothing else belongs in a build-type
         manifest — no application element, no activity, no attributes. Anything added
         here silently overrides main/ for release builds only, which is the hardest
         class of Android bug to reproduce.

         The claim is not verified by reading this file. It is verified by
         `aapt2 dump xmltree` against base/manifest/AndroidManifest.xml in the built AAB
         (the flag is spelled out in validate.yml; a double hyphen cannot appear inside
         an XML comment, which is a build failure and not a style point),
         because what ships is the MERGED manifest and the merged manifest can contain a
         permission that appears in none of our four files (SPEC.md §14 bullet 3). -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" tools:node="remove" />
</manifest>
